[[:db/retract 17592186304513 :answer-field/choices 17592186303456] [:db/retract 17592186304754 :answer-field/choices 17592186303207] [:db/retract 17592186304136 :answer-field/choices 17592186302687] [:db/retract 17592186308020 :answer-field/choices 17592186305547] [:db/retract 17592186307679 :answer-field/choices 17592186307213] [:db/retract 17592186307683 :answer-field/choices 17592186307080] {:db/id 17592186166912 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5d431d34-43bf-517f-a3a4-67b126780182" "question-677c9e5d-972e-57a9-95d7-9e9c75c0d58d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166536 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n![](images/0c/0c10626785034f566b65778fad63032e3a9ad8cc2c884bc3da3e88fdff3db50b.svg)\nwhere ![](images/18/18d25c282ee921144387d9c30e19a461746271a44cab929ab8075190b0f5c16e.svg)\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=4$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](images/e9/e99452a0ae70ad466de402da561590e4fc3b0de089fa0f74818a1986cadfbd42.svg)\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=-6$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](images/50/50b227b577144e091543a383e38d0d74a02193b8372fec1eddcfa75d06d3878b.svg)\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n![](images/0e/0ee79c93c2e488563f7515fb0ec6b30d5cb2b41795d23f0b9d4699338edcc92c.svg)\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/f1/f10a5247f1d1b5df56adab19d1dd64e9edbc04fb5615db365f62b1eebda40dc7.svg)\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since $e^{4t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/51/517dcfcf2467960dd5d35d9676c86c4bf0f66e0c4f13ccd99e862816efed675e.svg)\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since $e^{-6t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/91/91c074766b92790a0e78b768b0290d7723617d6ed30e51e842501cad63e58a44.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166913 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d20e4de9-7a35-5187-8874-829d308828ad"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166537 :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n![](images/50/50b547a3223fe85dde50b06d78ee35bfff49d95c43a4ebadcc2d1007ac041eb1.svg)\nwhere ![](images/ee/ee1bb1b6a9869493ae3bcfaa42d63d07b6054aaa19f25c9fa8a2665e9642e0be.svg)\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/85/853be349a48d204b073b1ad3aaa709df082bd7fa5163aec09d5364836918a9e6.svg)\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n![](images/59/5903874b33d77f242d725ad16fffef59ee24e1bde4210128ed590031ddc43e99.svg)\nmust approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, we must have $\\lambda_1 < 0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n![](images/85/856d5a86b76c30e0601803216465f29a1e3bd7fb993a58ee6ba85ba7c0627fd9.svg)\nmust also approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, we must have $\\lambda_2 < 0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies $\\lambda_1 < 0$ and $\\lambda_2 < 0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166914 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e04639b7-40d3-572d-8bb4-b806479b61ba" "question-92828ad5-f63d-58d9-843f-369518e02ad5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166542 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n![](images/cc/cc4019b4978cb96b931632ee2b6bf318b5eda46f4d9a73d252e76c7572285517.svg)\nwhere ![](images/45/458d9c56e8c1857546e7e112a9ad084fe7832781d448340ccfc141438a6eee78.svg)\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=-1$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](images/d5/d504ae47c4cfcd428a362d590d23713844d9ead5df792086a3bab07d41573bd8.svg)\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=-4$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](images/92/9210f84189ff4a94c1a1dded19ff6e409c75be9d4e27f17d4d1a4d9ff44fcc0b.svg)\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n![](images/38/38e227b8bac9c4cb2ae93e2b29e1ca70576383a176dcf193350a9b464a4dd88f.svg)\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/7e/7ef739bcc9a338d9535ab130a3bbe1cd46204bf7d8dbd55a49244abe9764a04a.svg)\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since $e^{-t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/92/924dcd7002fa679bf3a0021099928c45fba82a5bf54ba157a21d846eb1587640.svg)\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since $e^{-4t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions also approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/c5/c5b1aa6ccde39c56b503e2d59a56e2ac8058b08495567f4e2cb40f6a056faedd.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t) = c_1e^{-t}$ and $y(t)=c_2e^{-4t}$ provided that $c_1,c_2 \\neq 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{-4t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{-t})^{′}}=\\frac{-4{c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}}{-{c}_{1}{e}^{-t}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}}{{c}_{1}}{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as the trajectories approach the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/6c/6c3af2fe68c4bcddbbfd9f13eb84e73014511374c0adc570084f9c92773d31e0.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166915 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-637ffaff-c65f-5c9b-99c9-53a0db0e19a1" "question-decb29d1-761c-5045-8201-b6cd8aa2cc30" "question-2b822a97-2fd4-5c06-914f-271b7726501f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166540 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n![](images/73/73afc7cad8d4018e66ce31d26275c6d3238e0b50bfc85c65c84abb3d1d9c3c3c.svg)\nwhere ![](images/37/375392fe3e7223efb30963c4f51b76a08f868bdde210574bb8fb0f2e53f925be.svg)\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=2$ with the eigenvector ![](images/b8/b8dce83f78184c194c43b2cf2b4148549a4ff0504a0f663f6a338a8cca6dec2f.svg) The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/79/796f9e0b1c5698e87439547b5c84e00d492a6c7893cfd6adb793bd0639147d25.svg)\nlie on the $x$-axis and move away from the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{unstable}}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=4$ with the eigenvector ![](images/f9/f9da7bff2e0d90f112ce26291e3c1074e766e141bdae4198870d49136ed6706a.svg) The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/9d/9d8a16728992f0ce055be81c84ae0e905417e2ed74c4e659b277ab7f4226e90a.svg)\nlie on the $y$-axis and also move away from the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{unstable}}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{an unstable node (source)}}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/2b/2b79201d15fe5d2a9896cdee09c0474b97bb933d75fcf478c6fdb37cb4d16742.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-5d431d34-43bf-517f-a3a4-67b126780182" :question/id #uuid "5d431d34-43bf-517f-a3a4-67b126780182" :question/math-academy-id "q-332594" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=7y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=7$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{7t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{7t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{7t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/e9/e9fa4226361b8c0abe6c5b02374967b025aa8870757150f86b15434841825e2d.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cbcad781-5ef7-55c8-990d-9bd5fc7da67b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cbcad781-5ef7-55c8-990d-9bd5fc7da67b" :answer-field/id #uuid "cbcad781-5ef7-55c8-990d-9bd5fc7da67b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-876c7253-d60c-55be-9912-91c2e1235fbe" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1c5311f1-f33e-50da-9c1d-521aee7b5b70" "answer-aa7618f2-5fac-5ea0-beaa-eb999609c34e" "answer-29fe869b-63d9-585e-af7f-982e25668441" "answer-05b17d17-ec55-5e3f-b45a-f45e69858205" "answer-876c7253-d60c-55be-9912-91c2e1235fbe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c5311f1-f33e-50da-9c1d-521aee7b5b70" :answer/id #uuid "1c5311f1-f33e-50da-9c1d-521aee7b5b70" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/40/40fa7149639fce8388e4eedace5eaab60f38a4a43eca8367289cf6e1f8c0d25e.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aa7618f2-5fac-5ea0-beaa-eb999609c34e" :answer/id #uuid "aa7618f2-5fac-5ea0-beaa-eb999609c34e" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/41/41c2c33cd066a2350cb81f6a6b460c880e429ed6f9bb9901a18e06e3c61d0ae0.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29fe869b-63d9-585e-af7f-982e25668441" :answer/id #uuid "29fe869b-63d9-585e-af7f-982e25668441" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/b0/b01e4797a3de37e4fd92596b97c7736a066813d71f08a2c8282e70a5f597272d.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05b17d17-ec55-5e3f-b45a-f45e69858205" :answer/id #uuid "05b17d17-ec55-5e3f-b45a-f45e69858205" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/cd/cd695b56546593518654320ec7220ef4672d428592bb271b6f4a9fe5ad649cdf.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-876c7253-d60c-55be-9912-91c2e1235fbe" :answer/id #uuid "876c7253-d60c-55be-9912-91c2e1235fbe" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/e9/e9fa4226361b8c0abe6c5b02374967b025aa8870757150f86b15434841825e2d.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-677c9e5d-972e-57a9-95d7-9e9c75c0d58d" :question/id #uuid "677c9e5d-972e-57a9-95d7-9e9c75c0d58d" :question/math-academy-id "q-332597" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-6$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-6t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-6t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-6t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/b9/b99c7dc98a7a43dabb0fb7222fd6b4eeaaec727bc180953ee24769389667c501.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3dac8b30-2dc2-5060-873f-87232093db9b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3dac8b30-2dc2-5060-873f-87232093db9b" :answer-field/id #uuid "3dac8b30-2dc2-5060-873f-87232093db9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b6379650-ddd2-5b6c-b784-f66da29591d4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0a38f927-6500-530f-93d2-d5df8cb069c6" "answer-c2a0f343-8d44-511a-88de-660fe0869d8c" "answer-57abc8be-a071-5c7a-8dc2-b67f441e7b68" "answer-eb09e79d-18da-56f2-bb93-51310f53bbe1" "answer-b6379650-ddd2-5b6c-b784-f66da29591d4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0a38f927-6500-530f-93d2-d5df8cb069c6" :answer/id #uuid "0a38f927-6500-530f-93d2-d5df8cb069c6" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/30/309e2590fd90b31a57e618626821b1d2698b261e56bea67b8e87eca501d4e227.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c2a0f343-8d44-511a-88de-660fe0869d8c" :answer/id #uuid "c2a0f343-8d44-511a-88de-660fe0869d8c" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/47/4735969009a3c6c58af9c91ce7ee2e3572c52f777e967c666e7cba2792fd4c06.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-57abc8be-a071-5c7a-8dc2-b67f441e7b68" :answer/id #uuid "57abc8be-a071-5c7a-8dc2-b67f441e7b68" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/81/8149ed761a88317e708571e55047598f833cf7e5b806e5193b355947252f852c.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb09e79d-18da-56f2-bb93-51310f53bbe1" :answer/id #uuid "eb09e79d-18da-56f2-bb93-51310f53bbe1" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/a4/a484e0c8180f5805481ac71e47862cdcadbc32311f472e17cbd916be13bbfc04.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6379650-ddd2-5b6c-b784-f66da29591d4" :answer/id #uuid "b6379650-ddd2-5b6c-b784-f66da29591d4" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/b9/b99c7dc98a7a43dabb0fb7222fd6b4eeaaec727bc180953ee24769389667c501.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d20e4de9-7a35-5187-8874-829d308828ad" :question/id #uuid "d20e4de9-7a35-5187-8874-829d308828ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-332721" :question/problem "![](images/15/1515f63552e99b9ca9e1780f9689ac5d148969aaa1dccfc2ba55bcb87e534b34.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?" :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}<0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}<0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-7y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4e6571d0-2b85-5e20-ad0d-0458eb1f4ac3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4e6571d0-2b85-5e20-ad0d-0458eb1f4ac3" :answer-field/id #uuid "4e6571d0-2b85-5e20-ad0d-0458eb1f4ac3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-46bd9ac2-d6f5-56fc-b2fb-750eb4c39fd3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-46bd9ac2-d6f5-56fc-b2fb-750eb4c39fd3" "answer-2ff71483-521d-5e25-81d5-dd0d7513eda3" "answer-d71c1d37-aaf1-51f8-838a-ad2cd869f1c7" "answer-300406ec-c504-5656-92c4-2b5564e19ca3" "answer-d96d1f17-303b-53d2-98c9-2a8785c1198c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46bd9ac2-d6f5-56fc-b2fb-750eb4c39fd3" :answer/id #uuid "46bd9ac2-d6f5-56fc-b2fb-750eb4c39fd3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-7y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ff71483-521d-5e25-81d5-dd0d7513eda3" :answer/id #uuid "2ff71483-521d-5e25-81d5-dd0d7513eda3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=7y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d71c1d37-aaf1-51f8-838a-ad2cd869f1c7" :answer/id #uuid "d71c1d37-aaf1-51f8-838a-ad2cd869f1c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-7y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-300406ec-c504-5656-92c4-2b5564e19ca3" :answer/id #uuid "300406ec-c504-5656-92c4-2b5564e19ca3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=7y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d96d1f17-303b-53d2-98c9-2a8785c1198c" :answer/id #uuid "d96d1f17-303b-53d2-98c9-2a8785c1198c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=7x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e04639b7-40d3-572d-8bb4-b806479b61ba" :question/id #uuid "e04639b7-40d3-572d-8bb4-b806479b61ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-333189" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-5t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/df/df61bf017441f2dc3e34253d89f98f42cce2ec1b27131b9204952b059e335af8.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{-5t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{-3t})^{′}}=\\frac{-5{c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}}{-3{c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}}=\\frac{5{c}_{2}}{3{c}_{1}}{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as the trajectories approach the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/11/11484704b873e4f4cb449e8eb452e0e4e2ceb71d9f2918b052d5f18cbdd7ca7b.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ecccfb9f-b179-57da-b516-7c00246e405b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ecccfb9f-b179-57da-b516-7c00246e405b" :answer-field/id #uuid "ecccfb9f-b179-57da-b516-7c00246e405b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ac18ff8b-9d13-557c-8b66-d6ab72ff43d2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ac18ff8b-9d13-557c-8b66-d6ab72ff43d2" "answer-c25a7450-93cb-532e-9c59-fcdf3b4db26c" "answer-fbf68005-d553-5c2d-bdee-bb00a995e92d" "answer-8691b44a-b7c9-5764-a3ef-11415f5cb459" "answer-ac25791e-a8fe-5e48-85e4-e548f08e154a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac18ff8b-9d13-557c-8b66-d6ab72ff43d2" :answer/id #uuid "ac18ff8b-9d13-557c-8b66-d6ab72ff43d2" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/11/11484704b873e4f4cb449e8eb452e0e4e2ceb71d9f2918b052d5f18cbdd7ca7b.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c25a7450-93cb-532e-9c59-fcdf3b4db26c" :answer/id #uuid "c25a7450-93cb-532e-9c59-fcdf3b4db26c" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/53/538d3c19a9463e70595f02ccb93d35cfe2ac53c4755574f7e24b1fe6f8200bc6.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fbf68005-d553-5c2d-bdee-bb00a995e92d" :answer/id #uuid "fbf68005-d553-5c2d-bdee-bb00a995e92d" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/5a/5aa8ef49b3d81c5fc1fe5f6455f7081900078bfe6608bd63713dad68f6956e2a.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8691b44a-b7c9-5764-a3ef-11415f5cb459" :answer/id #uuid "8691b44a-b7c9-5764-a3ef-11415f5cb459" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/ba/badef95926770dce7b4b7bb7dd239d1d730acf1839b4af470a97365343bece58.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac25791e-a8fe-5e48-85e4-e548f08e154a" :answer/id #uuid "ac25791e-a8fe-5e48-85e4-e548f08e154a" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/dc/dc7abe688dfc7291cdbce67305c9651728c0ff9734949b1dde54c74d6c625192.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-92828ad5-f63d-58d9-843f-369518e02ad5" :question/id #uuid "92828ad5-f63d-58d9-843f-369518e02ad5" :question/math-academy-id "q-333188" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-5$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{-t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/36/362ee182f39f9d5cc1e7597f7e836a6ce802b4762a03c347c5701c0830f3d523.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-5t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{-3t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{-5t})^{′}}=\\frac{-3{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}}{-5{c}_{1}{e}^{-5t}}=\\frac{3{c}_{2}}{5{c}_{1}}{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to \\pm \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more vertical as the trajectories approach the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/bf/bff6e7e5f4772441cdc81c809eadc377d3c2c43bd653d0c5c4f7f306adf79a5e.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0521532e-d96e-5d0f-ae24-3ae545170424"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0521532e-d96e-5d0f-ae24-3ae545170424" :answer-field/id #uuid "0521532e-d96e-5d0f-ae24-3ae545170424" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-536c8ba2-9a76-58ee-a671-8d410c7b610d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cbc531d0-6c2d-5648-b378-65082454a6cf" "answer-e83d540c-00e0-5931-b515-134723ba6e69" "answer-7adc6eff-a523-5b7e-8d97-de46f768fe8b" "answer-d44e5877-6f35-50c1-a9af-6f5adefc791b" "answer-536c8ba2-9a76-58ee-a671-8d410c7b610d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cbc531d0-6c2d-5648-b378-65082454a6cf" :answer/id #uuid "cbc531d0-6c2d-5648-b378-65082454a6cf" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/07/0781cf2ca92efb10c050102b8dd034e004bb8da29b743fb154e2cc6aaf374c28.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e83d540c-00e0-5931-b515-134723ba6e69" :answer/id #uuid "e83d540c-00e0-5931-b515-134723ba6e69" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/13/139efbf21eb0cf93de2b041a63da84ac802898a810031c89471c620f0cb98ed6.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7adc6eff-a523-5b7e-8d97-de46f768fe8b" :answer/id #uuid "7adc6eff-a523-5b7e-8d97-de46f768fe8b" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/30/30d392d02e60c7bb8d76f7d4773a1d016d7cfa6343c893fb644428fde58571bd.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d44e5877-6f35-50c1-a9af-6f5adefc791b" :answer/id #uuid "d44e5877-6f35-50c1-a9af-6f5adefc791b" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/90/90addbe477ec66925586a5f8da3921dcaa6e22ed9ea4c1fb65f8b91095ad4850.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-536c8ba2-9a76-58ee-a671-8d410c7b610d" :answer/id #uuid "536c8ba2-9a76-58ee-a671-8d410c7b610d" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/bf/bff6e7e5f4772441cdc81c809eadc377d3c2c43bd653d0c5c4f7f306adf79a5e.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-637ffaff-c65f-5c9b-99c9-53a0db0e19a1" :question/id #uuid "637ffaff-c65f-5c9b-99c9-53a0db0e19a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-333211" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis and move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a saddle point}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/ae/ae1135571904a4bfb49eb79e187fa6d1754fc562fe66b604d33eb555418a1345.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0213fba8-0aae-5642-a445-07bb59e08746" "answer-field-1601144d-8231-50b4-86fa-aadedbf6075d" "answer-field-21385317-1cc7-529a-97be-528ef8b2772b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0213fba8-0aae-5642-a445-07bb59e08746" :answer-field/id #uuid "0213fba8-0aae-5642-a445-07bb59e08746" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-7728792e-46b5-5d38-8ba0-da19663ace00" :answer-field/choices ["answer-70d45c8d-cc13-5a1b-beb7-968053e2bd8e" "answer-7728792e-46b5-5d38-8ba0-da19663ace00"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70d45c8d-cc13-5a1b-beb7-968053e2bd8e" :answer/id #uuid "70d45c8d-cc13-5a1b-beb7-968053e2bd8e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "stable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7728792e-46b5-5d38-8ba0-da19663ace00" :answer/id #uuid "7728792e-46b5-5d38-8ba0-da19663ace00" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "unstable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1601144d-8231-50b4-86fa-aadedbf6075d" :answer-field/id #uuid "1601144d-8231-50b4-86fa-aadedbf6075d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-9f9f0a79-fb0f-5f4e-9fe8-53df6cb53d84" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9f9f0a79-fb0f-5f4e-9fe8-53df6cb53d84" "answer-d06ff763-ab36-521d-89ac-a4ece126dda9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f9f0a79-fb0f-5f4e-9fe8-53df6cb53d84" :answer/id #uuid "9f9f0a79-fb0f-5f4e-9fe8-53df6cb53d84" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "stable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d06ff763-ab36-521d-89ac-a4ece126dda9" :answer/id #uuid "d06ff763-ab36-521d-89ac-a4ece126dda9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "unstable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-21385317-1cc7-529a-97be-528ef8b2772b" :answer-field/id #uuid "21385317-1cc7-529a-97be-528ef8b2772b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-e4d54985-48c6-5b8f-be6d-43139558726a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e4d54985-48c6-5b8f-be6d-43139558726a" "answer-fa87b52e-dd41-5530-8584-7afc34ae6f23" "answer-edc9f0c6-e776-585d-b355-191e0404c0d6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4d54985-48c6-5b8f-be6d-43139558726a" :answer/id #uuid "e4d54985-48c6-5b8f-be6d-43139558726a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "a saddle point" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa87b52e-dd41-5530-8584-7afc34ae6f23" :answer/id #uuid "fa87b52e-dd41-5530-8584-7afc34ae6f23" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "a stable node (sink)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-edc9f0c6-e776-585d-b355-191e0404c0d6" :answer/id #uuid "edc9f0c6-e776-585d-b355-191e0404c0d6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an unstable node (source)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167200 :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186304513 :answer-field/correct 17592186303498 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186304754 :answer-field/correct 17592186302556 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186304136 :answer-field/correct 17592186303156 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186308410 :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186308020 :answer-field/correct 17592186306064 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186307679 :answer-field/correct 17592186305825 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186307683 :answer-field/correct 17592186306545 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-decb29d1-761c-5045-8201-b6cd8aa2cc30" :question/id #uuid "decb29d1-761c-5045-8201-b6cd8aa2cc30" :question/math-academy-id "q-333219" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis and move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis and also move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable node (source)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/29/29fba4c8d3fa024689c550da1bf54a50a81d73114ea1b2fa3a566966e9cb0692.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cf591f90-dd66-50c4-8cda-a517404994ce" "answer-field-66ebe660-063d-5f1d-940a-66eeeacdb12b" "answer-field-d666e47f-869a-5aac-9dd7-a42fc1d1dec8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cf591f90-dd66-50c4-8cda-a517404994ce" :answer-field/id #uuid "cf591f90-dd66-50c4-8cda-a517404994ce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-1ebb822a-d9bb-53f1-aa5e-f67d1d1606a8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d8f12fbf-ad1c-512d-8d0b-9e15adf9e998" "answer-1ebb822a-d9bb-53f1-aa5e-f67d1d1606a8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8f12fbf-ad1c-512d-8d0b-9e15adf9e998" :answer/id #uuid "d8f12fbf-ad1c-512d-8d0b-9e15adf9e998" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "stable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ebb822a-d9bb-53f1-aa5e-f67d1d1606a8" :answer/id #uuid "1ebb822a-d9bb-53f1-aa5e-f67d1d1606a8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "unstable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-66ebe660-063d-5f1d-940a-66eeeacdb12b" :answer-field/id #uuid "66ebe660-063d-5f1d-940a-66eeeacdb12b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-3f9fb416-e421-52c3-ac42-6432304298f6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b99fa12f-c90e-5ce0-a257-8f81c4970a5e" "answer-3f9fb416-e421-52c3-ac42-6432304298f6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b99fa12f-c90e-5ce0-a257-8f81c4970a5e" :answer/id #uuid "b99fa12f-c90e-5ce0-a257-8f81c4970a5e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "stable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f9fb416-e421-52c3-ac42-6432304298f6" :answer/id #uuid "3f9fb416-e421-52c3-ac42-6432304298f6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "unstable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d666e47f-869a-5aac-9dd7-a42fc1d1dec8" :answer-field/id #uuid "d666e47f-869a-5aac-9dd7-a42fc1d1dec8" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-9fdb3e9b-ea5e-59a0-a2dd-251d4fff7319" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b7431824-3f6b-583e-b9c7-63907ba033b3" "answer-a4d7a997-0b0c-5ae2-aa93-99cafbc24679" "answer-9fdb3e9b-ea5e-59a0-a2dd-251d4fff7319"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b7431824-3f6b-583e-b9c7-63907ba033b3" :answer/id #uuid "b7431824-3f6b-583e-b9c7-63907ba033b3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "a saddle point" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a4d7a997-0b0c-5ae2-aa93-99cafbc24679" :answer/id #uuid "a4d7a997-0b0c-5ae2-aa93-99cafbc24679" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "a stable node (sink)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9fdb3e9b-ea5e-59a0-a2dd-251d4fff7319" :answer/id #uuid "9fdb3e9b-ea5e-59a0-a2dd-251d4fff7319" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an unstable node (source)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2b822a97-2fd4-5c06-914f-271b7726501f" :question/id #uuid "2b822a97-2fd4-5c06-914f-271b7726501f" :question/math-academy-id "q-333226" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis and also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable node (sink)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1c/1c4133695eeb868f563f80021755fea75bef622e1ad46cbbf6daff1c953cb075.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e6148daf-4057-5d9a-8a64-641f2d763dc1" "answer-field-e14fbafa-f0bc-5fe2-bb94-563a8f48a64a" "answer-field-1af6e17b-3145-51a4-aeaa-9807cc90bb62"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e6148daf-4057-5d9a-8a64-641f2d763dc1" :answer-field/id #uuid "e6148daf-4057-5d9a-8a64-641f2d763dc1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-a5882e06-57dc-58e3-8a32-7bc5eed48bf7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a5882e06-57dc-58e3-8a32-7bc5eed48bf7" "answer-4e34e418-2968-5992-a5b6-638f9e13fb76"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5882e06-57dc-58e3-8a32-7bc5eed48bf7" :answer/id #uuid "a5882e06-57dc-58e3-8a32-7bc5eed48bf7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "stable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e34e418-2968-5992-a5b6-638f9e13fb76" :answer/id #uuid "4e34e418-2968-5992-a5b6-638f9e13fb76" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "unstable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e14fbafa-f0bc-5fe2-bb94-563a8f48a64a" :answer-field/id #uuid "e14fbafa-f0bc-5fe2-bb94-563a8f48a64a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-241c7c5a-1a6e-5bba-bd0d-81a296eeafbe" :answer-field/choices ["answer-241c7c5a-1a6e-5bba-bd0d-81a296eeafbe" "answer-dd242aa8-4bfe-5d24-b506-eff53329bbb7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-241c7c5a-1a6e-5bba-bd0d-81a296eeafbe" :answer/id #uuid "241c7c5a-1a6e-5bba-bd0d-81a296eeafbe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "stable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd242aa8-4bfe-5d24-b506-eff53329bbb7" :answer/id #uuid "dd242aa8-4bfe-5d24-b506-eff53329bbb7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "unstable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1af6e17b-3145-51a4-aeaa-9807cc90bb62" :answer-field/id #uuid "1af6e17b-3145-51a4-aeaa-9807cc90bb62" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-e714798a-3dc6-56b8-9999-84e5040e8804" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ee0863b6-4436-5460-b07a-e30f587eb499" "answer-e714798a-3dc6-56b8-9999-84e5040e8804" "answer-2d7c7175-1a31-52fd-87d7-275a7f4142ca"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee0863b6-4436-5460-b07a-e30f587eb499" :answer/id #uuid "ee0863b6-4436-5460-b07a-e30f587eb499" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "a saddle point" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e714798a-3dc6-56b8-9999-84e5040e8804" :answer/id #uuid "e714798a-3dc6-56b8-9999-84e5040e8804" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "a stable node (sink)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d7c7175-1a31-52fd-87d7-275a7f4142ca" :answer/id #uuid "2d7c7175-1a31-52fd-87d7-275a7f4142ca" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an unstable node (source)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168020 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a decoupled system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{1}x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{2}y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda_1>0$ and $\\lambda_2<0$ (the opposite case, where $\\lambda_1<0$ and $\\lambda_2>0,$ is similar). \n\nLet's now determine the behavior along the axes.\n\n- The straight-line solutions on the $x$-axis have the form ![](images/4d/4dc3aaf0f7dfd85070df84c13abe2cf02d85a704167569b3d3a76f2b58178c5b.svg) Since $\\lambda_1>0,$ we have that $e^{\\lambda_1 t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty.$ Thus, the trajectories on the $x$-axis move *away* from the origin. This is called an **unstable axis**.\n\n- The straight-line solutions on the $y$-axis have the form ![](images/76/76f6b9bbcdff2c87295fb0cd14dbdbbaa5e6c75eeb49c1cc158a4e8bd3da059d.svg) Since $\\lambda_2<0,$ we have that $e^{\\lambda_2 t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty.$ Thus, the trajectories on the $y$-axis move *toward* the origin. This is called a **stable axis**.\n\nBecause one axis is stable and the other is unstable, the origin (equilibrium point) is called a **saddle point**.\n\nIn the phase portrait, we draw\n\n- arrows on the $x$-axis pointing *outward* (away from the origin), while\n\n- arrows on the $y$-axis pointing *inward* (toward the origin).\n\nA typical trajectory bends from the stable direction and then peels away along the unstable direction.\n\n![](images/f2/f2538a59050e604d85fd9726e688cfbd2a5c9bdff4d15dd5ed465ed04f7290b4.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168002 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a decoupled system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{1}x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{2}y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2>0.$ \n\nLet's now determine the behavior along the axes.\n\n- The straight-line solutions on the $x$-axis have the form ![](images/75/75e7a0e16141db2a8da210c1ab30fc7328ccc98f861afec4db69d951ff3c9121.svg) Since $\\lambda_1>0,$ we have that $e^{\\lambda_1 t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty.$ Thus, the trajectories on the $x$-axis move *away* from the origin (*unstable axis*).\n\n- The straight-line solutions on the $y$-axis have the form ![](images/28/280e99a308962d43d1060be7100e294623d3bc3bfbe0579ab61d817695ad9e45.svg) Since $\\lambda_2>0,$ we have that $e^{\\lambda_2 t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty.$ Thus, the trajectories on the $y$-axis move *away* from the origin (*unstable axis*).\n\nBecause both axes are *unstable*, the origin (equilibrium point) is called an **unstable node** or **source**.\n\nIn the phase portrait, we draw:\n\n- arrows on the $x$-axis pointing *outward* (away from the origin), and\n\n- arrows on the $y$-axis pointing *outward* (away from the origin).\n\nA typical trajectory for an *unstable node* emanates from the origin toward infinity. \n\n![](images/c9/c9f315c0b8a60a2c00e2f8e8e8b954214ce4d82cfd5832e15c26b429e29e535e.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168004 :tutorial/content "Finally, let’s summarize what we’ve learned so far.\n\nAgain, we are considering a decoupled linear system\n![](images/e1/e1efdd0b8c037c01450042384ce3bac663d0b549fa9557d0db09b692754cab0f.svg)\n\nRecall that a homogeneous linear system has a single equilibrium point at the origin $(0,0).$ \nTo classify this equilibrium, we look at the signs of $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2.$\n\nFirst of all, the coordinate axes are straight-line solutions:\n\n- On the $x$-axis, we have $y(t)= 0$ and $x(t)=c_1e^{\\lambda_1 t}$.\n\n- If $\\lambda_1<0,$ the $x$-axis is a *stable* line.\n\n- If $\\lambda_1>0,$ the $x$-axis is an *unstable* line.\n\n- On the $y$-axis, we have $x(t)=0$ and $y(t)=c_2e^{\\lambda_2 t}.$\n\n- If $\\lambda_2<0,$ the $y$-axis is a *stable* line.\n\n- If $\\lambda_2>0,$ the $y$-axis is an *unstable* line.\n\nAs for the classification of the equilibrium at the origin, we get the following:\n\n- *Unstable node (source)* if $\\lambda_1>0$ and $\\lambda_2>0.$\n\n- *Stable node (sink)* if $\\lambda_1<0$ and $\\lambda_2<0.$ In fact, it's asymptotically stable, as the trajectories converge to the origin.\n\n- *Saddle point* if $\\lambda_1\\lambda_2<0$ (one positive, one negative).\n\nLet's see some examples." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167013 :activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
